  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Babu T.C vs Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Thursday, The 7Th Day Of January 2016/17Th Pousha, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 4427 of 2007 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 652/2006 of Sessions Court,Kozhikode St 2241/2001 of J.M.F.C.-V,Kozhikode Revision Petitioner(S)/Appellants/Accused:: -------------------------------------------------------------------- 1. Babu T.C., S/O. Nallan, Thekkidichalil House, Puthur P.O., Koduvally. 2. P. Ali, S/O. Mammed Koya Haji, Ansari Villa, Kinassery P.O., Pokkunnu Kozhikode-13 3. M/S. Swepna Condiments, 3.344 A, Iringallur P.O., G.A. College Kozhikode. By Adv. T.G.Rajendran Respondent(S)/Complainant & State:: ---------------------------------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Mobile Vigilance Squad, Kozhikode. 2. State Of Kerala, Rep. By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala Ernakulam. By Public Prosecutor, Shri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On Babu T.C vs Food Inspector on 7 January, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/117492290/ 1 07-01-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: B.Sudheendra Kumar, J. - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - -- - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - Crl.R.P. No.4427 of 2007 - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - -- - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - - Dated this the 7th day of January 2016 O R D E R",
    "facts": "The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 13.12.00 at about 12.30 p.m., PW1 the Food Inspector, Mobile Vigilance Squad, Kozhikode, purchased 400 m.l. of gingelly oil from the shop of the second revision petitioner. First revision petitioner was the salesman of the shop and the third revision petitioner was the firm. After sampling as per rules, one of the samples was sent for analysis to the Public Analyst. The Public Analyst filed Ext. P14 report stating that the sample did not conform to the standard prescribed for gingelly oil under the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1956 and therefore, the same was adulterated. Thereafter, on the request of the accused, second sample was sent to the Central Food Laboratory for analysis. Ext.P19 certificate of Central Food Laboratory would show that the sample did not conform to the standards prescribed for gingelly oil under the Act. It was further stated that the Iodine value and saponification value were less than the minimum prescribed limit and therefore the same was adulterated. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW3 were examined and Exts. P1 to P26 were marked for the prosecution. No evidence was adduced on the side of the revision petitioners.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P19 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext. P19 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioner relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioner and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
